---
// apps/site/src/pages/method.astro
// Public Method page — "How Verdecito works".
//
// Covers: minimal public ontology, AI use, privacy, render policy, lineage (anchor
// /method#lineage), tool acknowledgments. The deeper technical surface (full
// schema, conventions, migrations) stays in the repo for v0.1.
//
// This page intentionally does not duplicate /how-it-works.astro, which remains
// in the codebase as the older Explainer surface. TODO: decide whether to delete
// /how-it-works or redirect it to /method.

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description="How Verdecito works: minimal public ontology, privacy, render policy, lineage."
>
  <article style="margin-top:2rem">
    <div class="eyebrow">Method</div>
    <h1>How Verdecito Works</h1>
    <p class="lead">Method transparent. Content private by default.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      Verdecito is an open method and reference instance — not a closed app,
      not a black-box memory layer, not a database. It helps living work
      become visible, forkable, and inspectable, while keeping private content
      private and crediting what came before.
    </p>

    {/* TODO: image slot — verdecito_method_layers_privacy_lineage.png (prompt_ready). */}

    {/* ── Minimal public ontology ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h2>Minimal public ontology</h2>
      <p>For v0.1, the public vocabulary stays small.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Quest</strong> — a concrete current exploration with outputs,
          status, risks, and support needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Seed</strong> — a broader living possibility that may or may
          not become active work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sprout</strong> — a usable output, shown when it exists.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">
        Project, sprint, door, ask, artifact, lineage, trust level, Seed 000,
        tracker, regalito, and the deeper object model live in repo/docs rather
        than first public copy.
      </p>
      {/* TODO: when canonical site-config / ontology objects exist, render this
                list from them instead of from hard-coded copy. */}
    </section>

    {/* ── AI use ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h2>AI use</h2>
      <p>
        AI without clear context and values tends to optimize for whatever is
        easiest to produce: plausible text, productivity theater, generic plans,
        or hidden assumptions.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI is not good or bad by itself. It is a tool, and increasingly an
        unavoidable environment. But the provider, interface, defaults, memory
        layer, and business model can bias what it helps people become.
      </p>
      <p>
        Verdecito treats AI as a collaborator inside a transparent system, with
        humans on top and in control. The system matters: what context is
        allowed in, what values guide decisions, what becomes public, what stays
        private, what must be checked against reality, and what should be
        credited instead of reinvented.
      </p>
      <p style="font-style:italic;color:var(--muted);margin-top:1rem">
        Generic AI memory asks: "What should I remember?"<br />
        Verdecito asks: "What is this becoming, where does it belong, what
        should stay private, what already exists, and what could help it care
        for life better?"
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── Privacy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p style="font-weight:500">Transparent method. Private content.</p>
      <p>
        Verdecito can publish the method, the templates, the schemas, the
        render policy, and selected public examples without publishing
        someone's private identity, chats, notes, or unfinished inner work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Privacy is not an afterthought. It is part of the object model and
        render policy.
      </p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:1.25rem">Render policy summary</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Private objects do not reach the public surface.</li>
        <li>Teaser objects expose only title and summary.</li>
        <li>Public objects render in full.</li>
        <li>Required fields prevent silent corruption.</li>
        <li>Draft and archived objects stay out of the active public surface.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">
        For the canonical implementation, see
        {' '}<code>skins/verdecito/render-policy.yaml</code> and
        {' '}<code>apps/site/src/lib/verdecito.ts</code> in the repo.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── Lineage (anchor target: /method#lineage) ────────────────────── */}
    <section id="lineage" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h2>Lineage</h2>
      <p style="font-weight:500">Verdecito does not invent its parts. It composes them.</p>
      <p>
        Verdecito stands on older trenzas: regenerative and distributive
        economics, Buen Vivir and post-development, commons and OSS
        infrastructure, digital gardens and tools for thought, civic
        deliberation and collective intelligence, regenerative communities and
        bioregional coordination, and public-interest / trustworthy AI.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">
        Buen Vivir and Andean thought are living lineages, not decoration.
        Verdecito treats them as ancestry and influence, not aesthetic.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.25rem">Tool acknowledgments</h3>
      <p>Verdecito credits the tools it uses:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astro</a>
          {' '}— for the public site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
          {' '}and
          {' '}<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Pages</a>
          {' '}/
          {' '}<a href="https://github.com/sponsors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Sponsors</a>
          {' '}where applicable.
        </li>
        <li>Other OSS and public tools used in the stack.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">
        Note: privacy-friendly analytics (planned: GoatCounter, following prior
        GH Pages patterns) are not yet integrated and will only be acknowledged
        here once they are.
      </p>
      {/* TODO: when a canonical lineage object exists, render each lineage as a
                card linking to its best source. Tool acknowledgments could move
                into a canonical `acknowledgments` object. */}
    </section>

    {/* ── Repo door ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h2>Want the deeper technical layer?</h2>
      <p>
        Head to the repo for the spec, render policy, data model, license,
        issues, and current implementation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/verdecito-life/verdecito"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          class="btn btn-primary"
        >Open the repo</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--green-700);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--green-800);
    color: white;
  }
  h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
</style>
<!-- apps/site/src/pages/method.astro / Method page / anchor #lineage / v0.1 -->
